Step 2 Add Python to PATH

To add Python to the PATH environment variable in Windows 11, follow these steps:

  Locate Python Installation:

  -Find the directory where Python is installed. This typically looks like C:\Users\YourUsername\AppData\Local\Programs\Python\PythonXX (where XX is the version number, e.g., Python310).

  -You will also need the path to the Scripts folder within your Python installation, which will be something like C:\Users\YourUsername\AppData\Local\Programs\Python\PythonXX\Scripts.

  Open Environment Variables:

  Search for "environment variables" in the Windows Start menu.

  Select "Edit the system environment variables."

  In the System Properties window, click "Environment Variables..."

  Edit Path Variable:

  Under "System variables" (or "User variables" for your specific user), find the "Path" variable and click "Edit..."

  In the "Edit environment variable" window, click "New."

  Paste the path to your Python installation directory (e.g., C:\Users\YourUsername\AppData\Local\Programs\Python\Python310).

  Click "New" again and paste the path to your Python Scripts directory (e.g., C:\Users\YourUsername\AppData\Local\Programs\Python\Python310\Scripts).

  Click "OK" on all open windows to save the changes.

  Verify Installation:

  Open a new Command Prompt or PowerShell window.

  Type python --version and press Enter.

  The installed Python version should be displayed, confirming that Python is now accessible from the command line.

